{
  "item-handbooktext-lard": "Liquid animal fat, rendered by boiling chunks of fat in water. Its property of producing very little smoke when cooked and its flavor makes it ideal for frying foods. The fatty content makes dough more flaky, too.<br>Note: Cooking 1 chunk of fat results in 0.2L of lard, and most recipes in Expanded Foods only require 0.1L of lard - this effectively doubles how much fat you can use in recipes.",
  "item-handbooktext-limeegg": "Water-glassing preserves fresh eggs by submerging them in lime, which seals the eggshell and maintains its texture. Eggs can be water-glassed nearly indefinitely, but the lime must be regularly refreshed.",
  "item-handbooktext-maplesapportion": "Collected by placing a <a href=\"handbook://block-aculinaryartillery:spile-steel-north\">spile</a> into a maple log and a liquid container directly beneath it. The tree will only produce sap in January, Feburary, and March.",
  "item-handbooktext-meatnugget": "Meat can be tenderized via direct blunt force. Doing so makes it tastier and easier to digest. Chopped meat nuggets can be used in more foods and meals than tenderized meat.",
  "item-handbooktext-muffin": "A moist cupcake with a variety of flavors. By <a href=\"handbook://kneadingmixingguide\">kneading</a> soft dough with fruit, pumpkin, carrots, and/or gelatin, then baking the resulting batter, the muffin develops a browned crust that slightly caramelizes any exposed ingredients. It can then be topped with sweetener, such as honey, syrup, peanut sauce, or peanut butter.",
  "item-handbooktext-pasta": "An unleavened dough, cut into noodle form, then cooked and prepared with just about any ingredient. Fresh pasta is made by <a href=\"handbook://kneadingmixingguide\">kneading</a> flour and liquid egg, then flattening it into a sheet using an instrument. This dough sheet is then cut into noodles, which can be cooked over a firepit to make soup or hot pasta. Alternately, the noodles may be <a href=\"handbook://simmeringguide\">simmered</a> with water to cook them, after which they may be <a href=\"handbook://kneadingmixingguide\">mixed</a> with other ingredients, or added to stew.<br>Cut noodles can also be baked in an oven to dry them out into a more shelf-stable form, that can later be used anywhere the normal cut noodles can be.",
  "item-handbooktext-pasteurizedmilkportion": "Heating milk to slightly less than 100 °C will destroy the microorganisms within, reducing risk of disease and extending shelf life. The lack of bacteria makes it optimal for <a href=\"handbook://kneadingmixingguide\">kneading</a> with yogurt starter, so the yogurt's bacteria can ferment properly.",
  "item-handbooktext-peanut": "By roasting peanuts in an oven, their nutty flavor is enhanced and the skins become easier to remove. The roasted kernels are delicious and can be ground into very useful <a href=\"handbook://item-expandedfoods:peanutliquid-paste\">peanut paste</a>, while the leftover skins can be used as animal feed or fertilizer.",
  "item-handbooktext-peanutliquid-butter": "Made by <a href=\"handbook://kneadingmixingguide\">kneading</a> <a href=\"handbook://item-expandedfoods:peanutliquid-paste\">peanut paste</a> and cooking oil, the emulsified result is spreadable and high in calories. It can be sweetened further with honey or syrup, and salt makes all of the butter's flavors more pronounced.",
  "item-handbooktext-peanutliquid-paste": "Due to its high oil content, grinding peanuts results in a crunchy paste. This is edible enough on its own, but is a fantastic base for making <a href=\"handbook://item-expandedfoods:peanutliquid-butter\">peanut butter</a> or <a href=\"handbook://item-expandedfoods:peanutliquid-sauce\">peanut sauce</a>.",
  "item-handbooktext-peanutliquid-sauce": "A specialized sauce used mainly for dipping or topping food. Created by <a href=\"handbook://kneadingmixingguide\">kneading</a> <a href=\"handbook://item-expandedfoods:peanutliquid-paste\">peanut paste</a> with a savory sauce such as fish or soy sauce, a sweetener such as honey or syrup, and water. The result is a thick sauce with balanced flavors.",
  "item-handbooktext-pemmican": "An unappetizing, but palatable mixture of fat, meat, and fruit that lasts an extremely long time. Made by <a href=\"handbook://kneadingmixingguide\">kneading</a> fat or lard with fruit and meat nuggets. Unlike other foods, the sheer quantity of pemmican being created results in 2 chunks being output instead of 1. Mashed pemmican may be heated into a brick form and stored or eaten on its own, or it may be added to stews. Cooked pemmican may not be added to meals, as it is already cooked.",
  "item-handbooktext-plaindoughball": "Similar to regular <a href=\"handbook://item-dough-spelt\">dough</a>, but the addition of liquid egg has greatly changed the texture and composition.  In addition to <a href=\"handbook://kneadingmixingguide\">kneading</a> a liquid (such as broth, lard, milk, or water) with flour and liquid egg, adding bread starter and oil/lard results in a truly delectable bread treat. A fantastic base for making other foods.",
  "item-handbooktext-sausage": "A ground meat product that is preserved with fat. Basic sausage is made by <a href=\"handbook://kneadingmixingguide\">kneading</a> meat nuggets with fat or lard, but cheese makes an excellent filling. Adding salt during the kneading process will make curable sausage, which can be cured to last an extremely long time.<br>Raw (ie. non-curing) sausage may be hung from a <a href=\"handbook://block-aculinaryartillery:meathooks-ebony-tinbronze-east\">meat rack</a> in a manner similar to <a href=\"handbook://item-expandedfoods:agedmeat-redmeat-normal\">aged meat</a> in order to 'dry age' it. The aging process vastly improves its flavor and texture.",
  "item-handbooktext-soulstormbrew": "The highly addictive product of Projekt SoulStorm™.<br>Created by <a href=\"handbook://kneadingmixingguide\">kneading</a> a host of healthy, nutritious ingredients together to form the illustrious <a href=\"handbook://item-expandedfoods:soulstormbrew-slop\">SoulStorm Slop™</a>, an energy-rich solution that needs to be <a href=\"handbook://simmeringguide\">simmered</a> with noble, ancestral, and proprietary <a href=\"handbook://item-bonemeal\">ingredients</a> to make <a href=\"handbook://item-expandedfoods:soulstormbrew-refinedslop\">Refined SoulStorm Slop™</a>. At this point, the Slop™ can be distilled into pure, delicious, energizing <a href=\"handbook://item-expandedfoods:soulstormbrew-basic\">SoulStorm Brew™</a>!!!",
  "item-handbooktext-soulstormbrew2": "<font color=\"#77ad19\"><i>'THE TASTIEST ENERGY DRINK ON THE PLANET!!! DRINK, DRINK, DRINK!!!'</i></font>",
  "item-handbooktext-soymilk": "Place the soaked soybeans in your crafting grid to de-shell them, then combine with 1 L of water in the crafting grid to wet them. Grind the wettened soybeans in a quern to blend them, then juice the mixture in a fruit press to make soy milk.",
  "item-handbooktext-soyprep": "Creating <a href=\"handbook://item-expandedfoods:soysauce\">soy sauce</a> or <a href=\"handbook://item-expandedfoods:soymilk-edible\">soy milk</a> is a multi-step process. First, soak soybeans in water for 3 days. The next steps depend on the desired result.<br><br><strong>Making Soy Sauce</strong><br>Roast the soaked soybeans in an oven, then grind the roasted soybeans in a quern. Mix the soybean powder with any grain to culture it. Soak the cultured soybeans with brine for 1 week to make a soybean slurry, which can be juiced in a fruit press into soy sauce.<br><br><strong>Making Soy Milk</strong><br>Place the soaked soybeans in your crafting grid to de-shell them, then combine with 1 L of water in the crafting grid to wet them. Grind the wettened soybeans in a quern to blend them, then juice the mixture in a fruit press to make soy milk.",
  "item-handbooktext-soysauce": "Roast the soaked soybeans in an oven, then grind the roasted soybeans in a quern. Mix the soybean powder with any grain to culture it. Soak the cultured soybeans with brine for 1 week to make a soybean slurry, which can be juiced in a fruit press into soy sauce.",
  "item-handbooktext-stuffedpepper": "These giant peppers are well-suited for additional filling. In addition to bell pepper, take a basic ingredient, such as cheese, dried fruit, meat or vegetables, then <a href=\"handbook://kneadingmixingguide\">knead</a> it and bake the result.<br>Bell peppers may also be breaded. In addition to the bell pepper and basic ingredient, knead in a bonding agent such as food oil, honey, lard, liquid egg, or syrup, some flour to form a mild crust, and a handful of bread crumbs to finalize the breading. Breaded stuffed bell pepper can be further kneaded with lard or oil to enhance its flavor, then baked in an oven- but it may burn if left unattended. <a href=\"handbook://simmeringguide\">Simmering</a> it with lard or oil will result in a perfectly fried food every time, and cauldrons make it easy to fry in bulk.",
  "item-handbooktext-sushi": "A specialized food ideal for coastal societies. Rice and edible seaweed sheets are <a href=\"handbook://kneadingmixingguide\">kneaded</a>, with chopped vegetables and fish optionally added. Once rolled, additional toppings such as honey, tree syrup, or sauces may be drizzled for more flavor.",
  "item-handbooktext-trailmix": "A nutritious, long-lasting, easily stored snack mix. It provides almost every nutrition type, making it excellent for keeping your maximum HP up on a long journey.<br>Trail mix comes in four major varieties: Cheesy, fruity, meaty (or beany, for vegetarians), and nutty. <a href=\"handbook://kneadingmixingguide\">Knead</a> cheese, cured meat nuggets (or soybeans, for vegetarians), dried fruit, and nuts. The kind of trail mix that is made depends on the ingredient that makes up the majority of the recipe.",
  "item-handbooktext-treesyrupportion": "By boiling tree sap, the sugar to water ratio increases as water evaporates. This sweet condiment can be used as a topping for many foods and meals, or as a bonding agent for breading food.",
  "item-handbooktext-vegetablejuiceportion": "Made by juicing a variety of vegetables, this liquid can be used to make a variety of vegetable-flavored gelatins, or as stock in pilaf, hot pasta, or soup.",
  "item-handbooktext-yogurt": "Cultivating bacteria by fermenting curdled milk with fruit creates <a href=\"handbook://item-expandedfoods:yogurt-starter\">yogurt starter</a>, a highly active bacterial base. <a href=\"handbook://simmeringguide\">Simmering</a> the yogurt starter with pasteurized milk over a low heat will inoculate the bacteria, kickstarting the fermentation process and creating <a href=\"handbook://item-expandedfoods:yogurt-ferment\">fermenting yogurt</a>. Once this yogurt is allowed to ferment in a sealed barrel or a <a href=\"handbook://block-aculinaryartillery:bottle-clay-blue-corked\">bottle</a> until you have the final <a href=\"handbook://item-expandedfoods:yogurt-plain\">plain yogurt</a> product. At this point, plain yogurt may be <a href=\"handbook://kneadingmixingguide\">kneaded</a> with any kind of sweetener to make <a href=\"handbook://item-expandedfoods:yogurt-peach\">flavored yogurt</a>.<br>Plain or flavored yogurt can last almost indefinitely if stored properly - it will cure into yogurt starter instead of rot, and this yogurt starter can be used to repeatedly restart the above process.",
  "item-handbooktitle-acornportion": "Acorn Portion",
